      "settings": {
        "custom_liquid": "<script type=\"application/ld+json\">\n{\n  \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n  \"@graph\": [\n\t{\n  \t\"@type\": \"BlogPosting\",\n  \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/north-carolina-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#article\",\n  \t\"isPartOf\": {\n    \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/north-carolina-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#webpage\"\n  \t},\n  \t\"mainEntityOfPage\": {\n    \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/north-carolina-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#webpage\"\n  \t},\n  \t\"headline\": \"North Carolina Emergency Vehicle Light Laws: The Blue Light Possession Rule\",\n  \t\"description\": \"G.S. 20-130.1 makes possessing a blue light a crime in NC. Exception list, amber rules and what red lights do not grant. Reviewed September 2026.\",\n  \t\"abstract\": \"North Carolina General Statute § 20-130.1(c) makes it unlawful for any person to possess a blue light, as well as to install, activate or operate one, except for a publicly owned vehicle used for law enforcement purposes or any other vehicle when used by law enforcement officers in the performance of their official duties. Possession alone is the offense in North Carolina. Unlike most states, which regulate display or use, § 20-130.1(c) reaches the act of possessing the equipment. Violation of subsection (a) or (c) of North Carolina General Statute § 20-130.1 is a Class 1 misdemeanor. North Carolina General Statute § 20-130.1 defines \\\"red light\\\" to include any forward facing red light installed on a vehicle after initial manufacture, a definition added by Session Law 2013-415. A vehicle operated by any member of a municipal or rural fire department in the performance of that member's duties may display red lights in North Carolina under § 20-130.1(b)(8), regardless of whether members of that department are paid or voluntary. North Carolina Attorney General guidance states that private vehicles of fire department and rescue squad members are covered by G.S. 20-130.1 for red lights but are not included in G.S. 20-125, 20-145, 20-156 or 20-157, so they may use red lights without gaining speed, right of way or approach exemptions. North Carolina General Statute § 20-130.2 requires wreckers to carry an amber flashing light visible in all directions from 500 feet, activated at the scene of an accident or recovery operation and when towing a vehicle wider than 96 inches or wider than the towing vehicle.\",\n  \t\"speakable\": {\n    \t\"@type\": \"SpeakableSpecification\",\n    \t\"cssSelector\": [\n      \t\".key-takeaways\",\n      \t\"h1\"\n    \t]\n  \t},\n  \t\"inLanguage\": \"en-US\",\n  \t\"datePublished\": \"2026-08-DD\",\n  \t\"dateModified\": \"2026-08-DD\",\n  \t\"author\": {\n    \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/#organization\"\n  \t},\n  \t\"publisher\": {\n    \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/#organization\"\n  \t},\n  \t\"about\": [\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"Thing\",\n      \t\"name\": \"North Carolina emergency vehicle lighting law\"\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"Legislation\",\n      \t\"name\": \"N.C.G.S. §§ 20-130.1, 20-130.2\",\n      \t\"jurisdiction\": {\n        \t\"@type\": \"AdministrativeArea\",\n        \t\"name\": \"North Carolina\"\n      \t},\n      \t\"legislationJurisdiction\": \"US-NC\"\n    \t}\n  \t],\n  \t\"citation\": [\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"CreativeWork\",\n      \t\"name\": \"N.C.G.S. § 20-130.1 (North Carolina General Assembly)\",\n      \t\"url\": \"https://www.ncleg.net/enactedlegislation/statutes/html/bysection/chapter_20/gs_20-130.1.html\"\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"CreativeWork\",\n      \t\"name\": \"N.C.G.S. § 20-130.2 (North Carolina General Assembly)\",\n      \t\"url\": \"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/BySection/Chapter_20/GS_20-130.2.pdf\"\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"CreativeWork\",\n      \t\"name\": \"North Carolina Department of Justice, Motor Vehicles: Use of Special Warning Equipment\",\n      \t\"url\": \"https://ncdoj.gov/opinions/motor-vehicles-use-of-special-warning-equipment-rules-of-the-road/\"\n    \t}\n  \t],\n  \t\"image\": {\n    \t\"@type\": \"ImageObject\",\n    \t\"url\": \"REPLACE_WITH_FEATURED_IMAGE_URL\",\n    \t\"width\": 1200,\n    \t\"height\": 630\n  \t}\n\t},\n\t{\n  \t\"@type\": \"WebPage\",\n  \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/north-carolina-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#webpage\",\n  \t\"url\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/north-carolina-emergency-vehicle-light-laws\",\n  \t\"name\": \"North Carolina Emergency Vehicle Light Laws (2026 Guide)\",\n  \t\"description\": \"G.S. 20-130.1 makes possessing a blue light a crime in NC. Exception list, amber rules and what red lights do not grant. Reviewed September 2026.\",\n  \t\"isPartOf\": {\n    \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/#website\"\n  \t},\n  \t\"breadcrumb\": {\n    \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/north-carolina-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#breadcrumb\"\n  \t},\n  \t\"lastReviewed\": \"2026-08-DD\",\n  \t\"reviewedBy\": {\n    \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/#organization\"\n  \t},\n  \t\"inLanguage\": \"en-US\"\n\t},\n\t{\n  \t\"@type\": \"BreadcrumbList\",\n  \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/north-carolina-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#breadcrumb\",\n  \t\"itemListElement\": [\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"ListItem\",\n      \t\"position\": 1,\n      \t\"name\": \"Home\",\n      \t\"item\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/\"\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"ListItem\",\n      \t\"position\": 2,\n      \t\"name\": \"Informative Blogs\",\n      \t\"item\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative\"\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"ListItem\",\n      \t\"position\": 3,\n      \t\"name\": \"North Carolina Emergency Vehicle Light Laws\"\n    \t}\n  \t]\n\t},\n\t{\n  \t\"@type\": \"FAQPage\",\n  \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/north-carolina-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#faq\",\n  \t\"mainEntity\": [\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \t\"name\": \"Is it illegal to own a blue light in North Carolina?\",\n      \t\"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \t\"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \t\"text\": \"Yes, in most circumstances. North Carolina General Statute § 20-130.1(c) makes it unlawful for any person to possess a blue light, or to install, activate or operate one in or on any vehicle, except for a publicly owned vehicle used for law enforcement purposes or any other vehicle when used by law enforcement officers in the performance of their official duties. Violation is a Class 1 misdemeanor.\"\n      \t}\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \t\"name\": \"Can volunteer firefighters use red lights in North Carolina?\",\n      \t\"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \t\"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \t\"text\": \"Yes. G.S. § 20-130.1(b)(8) covers a vehicle operated by any member of a municipal or rural fire department in the performance of that member's duties, regardless of whether members of the department are paid or voluntary.\"\n      \t}\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \t\"name\": \"Do North Carolina red lights give right of way?\",\n      \t\"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \t\"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \t\"text\": \"No. North Carolina Department of Justice guidance notes that private vehicles of fire department and rescue squad members are included in G.S. 20-130.1 for red lights but not in G.S. 20-125, 20-145, 20-156 or 20-157, so they gain no siren, speed, right of way or approach exemptions.\"\n      \t}\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \t\"name\": \"What amber light does a North Carolina wrecker need?\",\n      \t\"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \t\"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \t\"text\": \"G.S. § 20-130.2 requires an amber-colored flashing light mounted so as to be clearly visible in all directions from 500 feet, activated at the scene of an accident or recovery operation and when towing a vehicle wider than 96 inches or wider than the towing vehicle.\"\n      \t}\n    \t}\n  \t]\n\t},\n\t{\n  \t\"@type\": \"Organization\",\n  \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/#organization\",\n  \t\"name\": \"Strobes N' More\",\n  \t\"url\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/\",\n  \t\"telephone\": \"+1-401-454-4487\",\n  \t\"email\": \"sales@strobesnmore.com\",\n  \t\"address\": {\n    \t\"@type\": \"PostalAddress\",\n    \t\"streetAddress\": \"81 Tom Harvey Road\",\n    \t\"addressLocality\": \"Westerly\",\n    \t\"addressRegion\": \"RI\",\n    \t\"postalCode\": \"02891\",\n    \t\"addressCountry\": \"US\"\n  \t}\n\t},\n\t{\n  \t\"@type\": \"WebSite\",\n  \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/#website\",\n  \t\"url\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/\",\n  \t\"name\": \"Strobes N' More\",\n  \t\"publisher\": {\n    \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/#organization\"\n  \t}\n\t}\n  ]\n}\n</script>"
